---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');

// ─── Every letter and message, and what has come back ─────────────────────────
const letters = [
  ['14 Sep', 'Arquivo da Defesa Nacional', 'Paço d’Arcos', 'Henrique’s 1961 military registration', 'Delivered'],
  ['14 Sep', 'Arquivo Histórico Militar', 'Lisboa', 'Same request, after the bounce', 'Acknowledged'],
  ['14 Sep', 'Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique', 'Maputo', 'Recruitment-district records, 1950s–60s', 'Delivered'],
  ['15 Sep', 'Arquivo Histórico de Moçambique', 'Maputo', 'Ivone’s 1924 birth certificate', 'Delivered'],
  ['15 Sep', 'Master of the High Court', 'South Gauteng', 'J294 in estate 12294/2023 — Henrique', 'Delivered'],
  ['15 Sep', 'Landesarchiv Berlin', 'Berlin', 'Gertrud’s death 1943 · the 1924 marriage and divorce · Kurt’s 1912 birth entry', 'Delivered'],
  ['15 Sep', 'National Archives', 'Pretoria', 'J294 in estate TAB MHG 7584/69 — Kurt', 'Delivered'],
  ['15 Sep', '1ª Conservatória do Registo Civil', 'Porto', 'Ivone’s death record, date unknown', 'Delivered'],
  ['15 Sep', 'Archiwum Państwowe', 'Bydgoszcz', 'Standesamt Schubin 1884 — Jacob’s birth', 'Acknowledged'],
  ['15 Sep', 'Archiwum Państwowe', 'Koszalin', 'Standesamt Hammerstein 1884 — Georg; and Sally, 1847', 'Delivered'],
  ['14 Sep', 'Arquivo Histórico Militar', '—', 'Sent to a dead domain', 'Bounced — resent'],
  ['15 Sep 09:27', 'The 292.7 cM match', 'MyHeritage', 'Her own Castles in the Baldock censuses, and one question back: what became of the Castle sons', 'Sent'],
  ['15 Sep 09:28', 'The 297.5 cM match', 'MyHeritage', 'A correction — the link is her grandmother, not her grandfather', 'Sent'],
  ['15 Sep 12:01', 'The keeper of the Nel Web Site', 'MyHeritage', 'Leonard’s English family, and the finding. The first cold letter of the project', 'Sent'],
  ['15 Sep', 'Master of the High Court', 'Cape Town', 'J294 in estates 10761/2024 and 018561/2021 — the two Wear daughters who died in the Cape', 'Delivered'],
];

// ─── The fifteen, with live state ─────────────────────────────────────────────
const S = {
  sent:    ['sent',    'Sent — awaiting reply'],
  ack:     ['ack',     'Acknowledged'],
  you:     ['you',     'Waiting on the family'],
  blocked: ['blocked', 'Not doable from here'],
};
const items = [
  [1,  'Order estate 12294/2023 — Henrique’s J294', S.sent, 'Sent to the named estate controller for files ending in 4, with a line asking her to redirect it if the estate sits elsewhere.'],
  [2,  'Order estate 7584/69 — Kurt’s J294', S.sent, 'Corrected mid-flight: this is a National Archives request, not a Master’s one. Kurt died in 1968 and files that old went to Pretoria long ago.'],
  [3,  'Ivone’s Porto death record', S.sent, 'A false claim of kinship was caught in the draft and removed before sending. Asks whether a search by name is possible at all, since her date of death is unknown.'],
  [4,  'Ivone’s 1924 Lourenço Marques birth', S.sent, 'Her 1924 birth falls inside the Maputo archive’s 1865–1934 range — it was Henrique’s 1941 that fell outside. Asks for parents and, if recorded, grandparents.'],
  [5,  'Henrique’s 1968 marriage record', S.blocked, 'Searched on both groom and bride. Not in FamilySearch — the South African marriage collections thin out after the mid-1960s. May simply arrive inside the estate file at item 1.'],
  [6,  'The June 1948 ante-nuptial contract', S.blocked, 'A South African deeds registry “may not give out information acting on a letter or a telephone call.” Needs attendance in person or a paid commercial search.'],
  [7,  'The February 1956 ante-nuptial contract', S.blocked, 'Same office, same obstacle.'],
  [8,  'Write to the closest British match', S.ack, 'SENT 15 September, then CORRECTED an hour later. The 1952 register\'s birthplace column shows Mervin Halsey was born in the Cape Province and Cynthia Wear in England, so the link is the grandmother. A second message told her so. And the question the first letter asked has since answered itself anyway: the verified South African death records give HENRY GEORGE CHARLES HALSEY, 1906-1993, and MARY ELLEN HALSEY, 1907-1981 — the right ages to be Mervin\'s parents. Untested, and no longer on the critical path.'],
  [9,  'Write to the keeper of the 10,715-person tree', S.ack, 'SENT 15 September as a follow-up inside the June 2025 thread — and again at 09:27 the same day, this time carrying a finding rather than a question: her own Castle family in the Baldock censuses, two children her tree is missing, and the correction that Caroline was born a Walls. The question back is narrow — what became of the Castle sons, and did any of them go to South Africa.'],
  [10, 'Big Y-700 on Derrick’s kit', S.you, 'A purchase. This archive does not spend other people’s money.'],
  [11, 'Kurt’s naturalisation', S.blocked, 'Done as far as it can be. The Union gazette did publish naturalisation applications — his partner made one in February 1942 — and Kurt appears in none of them under any spelling the OCR catches. Recorded as a negative with its limits.'],
  [12, 'Finish the Gazettes.Africa sweep', S.blocked, 'Done. Leibholz zero, Salkow zero, “Piccadilly Hat” nothing after 1952, Walter Oppenheimer seven including his naturalisation, Ivy Fuchs seven including her husband’s estate.'],
  [13, 'Gertrud’s death register, 27 February 1943', S.sent, 'Asked as one of three questions in the Berlin letter. The cause of death is the point.'],
  [14, 'Auguste and Jacob’s divorce file', S.sent, 'Same letter. In a Berlin Mischehe the date is the whole meaning of the act.'],
  [15, 'Szubin and Hammerstein registers', S.ack, 'Two letters, one to each archive, each told what the other was asked. Both civil registries opened in 1874, so an 1884 birth entry names both parents outright.'],
];

const count = (k) => items.filter(i => i[2][0] === k).length;
---
<Base title="Status"
  subtitle="Every open thread in one place, and the date it last moved"
  lede="This page exists because the answer to “are you keeping track of all this?” was honestly: in four places, and the published one was already stale. This is the one that is not.">

  <div class="cards">
    <div class="card stat"><b>{letters.filter(l => l[4] !== 'Not sent').length}</b><span>Out the door</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{letters.filter(l => l[4] === 'Acknowledged').length}</b><span>Acknowledged</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{count('sent') + count('ack')}</b><span>In flight</span></div>
    <div class="card stat"><b>{count('you')}</b><span>Waiting on you</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="next15">The next fifteen <span class="tag doc">Set 15 September, evening</span></h2>
  <p class="small">
    The position changed twice today — first when the segments named a couple, then when a source
    that indexes women by their <em>maiden</em> name turned up. Both open work that was not available
    this morning. These are in the order they would change the answer.
  </p>

  <h3>Documents that would settle it</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td><strong>Leonard&rsquo;s own estate.</strong> Sweep the gazettes 1973&ndash;1982 surname-first, and NAAIRS for an MHG file. His death notice names <em>his</em> parents <em>and his children</em> — the single most useful page in this whole enquiry. It also settles 1973 against 1979.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>MHG 10245/69 — Henry Robert Wear&rsquo;s estate</strong>, National Archives, Pretoria. Its death notice lists his children, which tests the entire Letchworth reconstruction against a South African document rather than an English index.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td><strong>WLD 1950/1948</strong> — Kurt against Ivone, filed within months of marrying her — with <strong>497/1951</strong> and <strong>2368/1951</strong> behind it. Why he filed is the question this archive has circled since the beginning.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td><strong>Willem Adrian Nel&rsquo;s estate.</strong> He predeceased Gillian, so his death notice names <strong>their children</strong> — which is to say, whether Derrick has living half-nieces and nephews who could still take a test. Free, in the gazettes, surname first.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>DNA work now possible that was not this morning</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td><strong>Cross-match the other two top matches</strong> — 333.9 and 165.9 cM. Their shared-match tables were never read, and it was reading the first two that broke this open.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td><strong>Segment-test those two against the 297.5 match.</strong> Does the 165.9 match share <em>Wear</em> segments or only <em>Castle</em> ones? The archive has been <em>assuming</em> he is Brown-side. The chromosome browser can say.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>7</strong></td><td><strong>Re-sweep all 5,464 matches</strong> for <strong>Wear</strong>, <strong>Robertson</strong>, <strong>Nel</strong> and <strong>Green</strong>. Castle returned fourteen; Wear returned eight and the top one was a seventeenth-century <em>Weare</em>. Now the archive knows which is which.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>The shape of the Wear family</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>8</strong></td><td><strong>Is Henry Robert Wear in the 1939 Register?</strong> If the household is absent from England in September 1939, they had already gone — which dates the crossing, and the archive currently cannot date it at all.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>9</strong></td><td><strong>Outbound passenger lists, Wear to South Africa, 1936&ndash;1948.</strong> This was hopeless when the surname was a guess. It is tractable now: the family, the ages and the destination are all known.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>10</strong></td><td><strong>Did Bruce Wallace Wear have sons?</strong> Four marriages. A son carries the <strong>Wear Y-chromosome</strong>, and one Y test from him would confirm or destroy this entire reading in a single result. South African births and marriages, 1960&ndash;1985.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>11</strong></td><td><strong>Bruce&rsquo;s own death.</strong> Not in the gazettes, not in the verified deaths. Try the Cape rather than the Transvaal — Kuruman is Northern Cape — and the 1984 and 2004 voter rolls.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>Loose ends, and one debt</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>12</strong></td><td><strong>Mervin Halsey&rsquo;s parents.</strong> <strong>Henry George Charles Halsey</strong> 1906&ndash;1993 and <strong>Mary Ellen Halsey</strong> 1907&ndash;1981 are the right ages. Their gazette estates would settle it and close a question this archive carried for a fortnight before it went moot.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>13</strong></td><td><strong>Re-run every woman in the archive by maiden name</strong>, now that it is possible: Auguste&rsquo;s sister Hilda; Kurt&rsquo;s other wives Frances Hostowsky and Pauline Rappoport; and <strong>Ivone under Salkow</strong> — her Porto death has defeated every Portuguese route, and it may be reachable from the South African side.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>14</strong></td><td><strong>The gendatabase terms.</strong> Their pages say their information may not be republished without written permission, and this archive is on the open web. Ask through their form, re-source the five facts elsewhere, or move them into the working notes. <strong>Currently it is publishing against a stated term.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>15</strong></td><td><strong>Read the 1948 register at full resolution.</strong> The 1956 register was read that way and produced the Piccadilly Hat Company; the 1952 was read that way and redirected the whole archive. <strong>The 1948 one — Kurt and Ivone, certificate 858 — never has been.</strong></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2 id="plan">The plan, 15 September <span class="tag doc">Rewritten after the gendatabase sweep</span></h2>
  <p class="small">
    A South African search engine the family found, <strong>gendatabase.com</strong>, indexes women
    under their <strong>maiden</strong> name — the one thing nothing else here could do. Sweeping every
    surname in this archive through it took an hour and changed the shape of what is left to do.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Documents to order, in the order they matter</h3>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Reference</th><th>What it would settle</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>1</strong></td><td><strong>WLD 1950/1948</strong><br/><span class="small muted">National Archives, Pretoria</span></td><td><strong>Kurt Luwinsky versus Ivone, born Mota Figueiredo — 1948.</strong> He married her in June and filed the same year. This archive has circled that fact for weeks without a file number. <strong>The grounds are in this file.</strong></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>2</strong></td><td><strong>WLD 497/1951</strong> and <strong>WLD 2368/1951</strong></td><td>Restitution of conjugal rights, then divorce — the South African sequence for desertion. Why the 1948 action did not finish it.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>3</strong></td><td><strong>MHG 10245/69</strong></td><td><strong>Henry Robert Wear&rsquo;s own estate.</strong> Its death notice names his children — which tests the whole Letchworth reconstruction against a South African document.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>4</strong></td><td><strong>MHG 7584/69</strong></td><td>Kurt&rsquo;s estate. Already requested from Pretoria; still waiting.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>5</strong></td><td><strong>12294/2023</strong></td><td>Henrique&rsquo;s estate. Already requested; still waiting.</td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>6</strong></td><td><strong>WLD 790/1957</strong></td><td>Wear versus Norquoy, six years after the divorce. Applications of that kind are usually about children.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="small muted">&mdash;</td><td class="small muted"><s>33157/2014</s></td><td class="small muted"><strong>Cancelled.</strong> Edwina was born a Green. She is nobody.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h3>2. What a paid gendatabase account buys — and what it does not</h3>
  <div class="panel">
    <p>
      <strong>Checked rather than assumed.</strong> The free profile says so itself: <em>&ldquo;dates
      show the year only and fields marked Members only are hidden.&rdquo;</em> Membership runs
      <strong>R110 / R245 / R400</strong> — roughly &pound;4.60, &pound;10, &pound;17 — and a single
      verified report is <strong>R20</strong>, about 85p.
    </p>
    <p>
      A paid report gives full names and maiden name, <strong>full date of birth</strong>,
      <strong>identity number</strong>, citizenship, last marital status, living status, and sometimes
      address and telephone history.
    </p>
    <p class="verdict disp">
      <strong>Corrected within the hour.</strong> That was written from the marketing copy and it was
      too flat. Tested on a profile whose estate number is already known from the gazette, the hidden
      fields are: identity number, old identity number, marriage details, name changes, the
      <strong>1984 and 2004 voter rolls</strong>, place and cause of death — and
      <strong>Estate Information</strong>.
    </p>
    <blockquote>
      Estate information is the one that matters. It does not give parents. <strong>It gives the estate
      reference — and the death notice inside an estate file names the father and the mother.</strong>
      gendatabase &rarr; estate number &rarr; order the file &rarr; parents. That is exactly the chain
      this archive needs, one step removed. <strong>Worth the R110.</strong>
    </blockquote>
    <p class="small">
      The lookups that would pay for it on the first day: <strong>Gillian Agnes Louisa Nel</strong>
      (1941&ndash;2024) — is Leonard her father; <strong>Leonard</strong> himself — his parents, his
      heirs, and whether he died in 1973 or 1979; the three other <strong>Wear</strong>-born daughters
      of 1943, 1946 and 1949; and <strong>Mervin Richard Halsey</strong>, which would close a question
      this archive has carried for a fortnight.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And then the paywall turned out not to matter <span class="tag doc">Settled, free</span></h3>
    <p>
      Every South African estate over the threshold must be advertised as a <strong>Form J193 notice in
      the Government Gazette</strong>, and that corpus is free, full-text, and runs to 2026.
      <strong>The estate numbers were never behind a paywall at all.</strong> The only trick is the name
      order — modern notices read <em>surname first</em>, so &ldquo;Gillian Agnes Louisa Nel&rdquo;
      returns nothing and &ldquo;Gillian Agnes Louisa&rdquo; finds her immediately.
    </p>
    <div class="scroll"><table>
      <thead><tr><th>Born Wear</th><th>Estate</th><th>Born</th><th>Died</th><th>Master</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Gillian Agnes Louisa Nel</strong></td><td><strong>10761/2024</strong></td><td><strong>8 Aug 1941</strong></td><td>17 Mar 2024, Hermanus</td><td>Cape Town</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Patricia Mary Maud Grant</strong></td><td><strong>018561/2021</strong></td><td><strong>23 Mar 1946</strong></td><td>7 Jun 2021, Pinelands</td><td>Cape Town</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Heather Jill Hunter</td><td><strong>017256/2022</strong></td><td>1949</td><td>2022</td><td>Durban</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Doris Dawn Joubert</td><td><strong>015639/2022/DBN</strong></td><td>1943</td><td>2022</td><td>Durban</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p>
      Gillian&rsquo;s notice adds her exact birth date, her identity number, her address at Hermanus,
      and a blank where a surviving spouse would go. <strong>Two of these four retired to the Cape and
      died under the same Master.</strong> Derrick was born between the second and the third.
    </p>
    <p class="verdict doc">
      <strong>So: do not buy the subscription for this.</strong> It would still reach people the
      gazettes cannot — chiefly <strong>Leonard himself</strong>, who died in 1973 or 1979 where the
      OCR is poor — and it would add identity numbers and the 1984 and 2004 voter rolls. That is worth
      R110 to somebody. It is not needed here. <strong>The next step is a letter, not a payment:</strong>
      the Master of the High Court at Cape Town, for <strong>10761/2024</strong> and
      <strong>018561/2021</strong>. Each file holds a death notice with a field for the father&rsquo;s
      name.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And a condition this archive has to meet <span class="tag disp">Unresolved</span></h3>
    <p>
      Every gendatabase profile carries this: <em>&ldquo;Information found on GenDatabase may not be
      shared online or in print without prior written permission.&rdquo;</em>
      <strong>This archive is published on the open web.</strong>
    </p>
    <p class="small">
      The National Archives references — the 1948 and 1951 court files, the estate numbers — are
      <strong>not</strong> affected: NAAIRS is a free public index and this archive queried it directly.
      But a handful of facts here came from gendatabase and nowhere else — Edwina&rsquo;s maiden name,
      Gillian&rsquo;s dates, Leonard&rsquo;s sisters by married name, his 1979 death. Either permission
      is asked for, or those facts are re-sourced elsewhere, or they come off the public pages and stay
      in the working notes. <strong>Recorded here rather than left for somebody to notice.</strong>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h3>3. Still free, still undone</h3>
  <ul class="small">
    <li>Sweep the remaining surnames — <strong>Castle</strong> (5,454, needs narrowing), Wallis, Brister, Parr, Fenton, Webb, Harrison, Green, Thorp.</li>
    <li>Test whether <strong>Henry George Charles Halsey</strong> (1906&ndash;1993) and <strong>Mary Ellen Halsey</strong> (1907&ndash;1981) are Mervin&rsquo;s parents — the question put to the closest match this morning, before it went moot.</li>
    <li>Settle Leonard&rsquo;s death year: the Nel tree says <strong>1973</strong>, the verified death database says <strong>1979</strong>. Two secondary sources disagreeing.</li>
    <li>Test the three other <strong>Wear</strong>-born daughters of the 1940s — 1943, 1946, 1949 — against the other Transvaal Wear family.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The letters</h2>
  <p class="small">
    Eleven sent, and <strong>exactly one has ever bounced</strong> — the one sent before every address
    was checked for a mail record first. That is now standing procedure, and it has since caught three
    dead addresses before they cost anything.
  </p>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>Sent</th><th>To</th><th>Where</th><th>Asking for</th><th>State</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {letters.map(([d, who, where, what, state]) => (
        <tr>
          <td class="small">{d}</td>
          <td><strong>{who}</strong></td>
          <td class="small">{where}</td>
          <td class="small">{what}</td>
          <td><span class={'tag ' + (state === 'Acknowledged' ? 'doc' : state.startsWith('Bounced') ? 'disp' : state === 'Not sent' ? 'open' : 'inf')}>{state}</span></td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Item 8 answered itself, 15 September <span class="tag doc">Closed</span></h3>
    <p>
      The letter asking the closest match who her grandfather&rsquo;s parents were went out in the
      morning. By the afternoon the question was moot: a column on the 1952 marriage register shows
      her grandfather was born in the <strong>Cape Province</strong> and her grandmother in
      <strong>England</strong>, and the English side is the one that matters. Her grandmother&rsquo;s
      birth entry names the mother as a <strong>Castle</strong>, of <strong>Baldock,
      Hertfordshire</strong> — the second of Derrick&rsquo;s top matches descends from the same couple
      through a different daughter. <a href={base + '/dna/#castle'}>The chain →</a>
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The fifteen</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th></th><th>Action</th><th>State</th><th>Where it stands</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      {items.map(([n, what, [k, label], note]) => (
        <tr>
          <td><strong>{n}</strong></td>
          <td><strong>{what}</strong></td>
          <td><span class={'tag ' + (k === 'ack' ? 'doc' : k === 'you' ? 'open' : k === 'blocked' ? 'disp' : 'inf')}>{label}</span></td>
          <td class="small">{note}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table></div>

  <h2>Two messages sent on the finding itself <span class="tag doc">15 September, 09:27 and 09:28</span></h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>To</th><th>Carrying</th><th>Asking</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>The 292.7 cM match</strong><br/><span class="small muted">keeper of the 10,715-person tree</span></td>
        <td>Her own Castles, found in the 1901 and 1911 censuses at Baldock — including <strong>two children her tree does not have</strong>, Hilda b.1902 and Ernest b.1904, and the correction that Caroline was born a <strong>Walls</strong>, her widowed father Joseph living in the house.</td>
        <td><strong>What became of the Castle sons</strong> — George W. b.1885, Frederick b.1890, Ernest b.1904 — and whether any of them, or their children, went out to South Africa.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>The 297.5 cM match</strong><br/><span class="small muted">a correction, an hour after the fact</span></td>
        <td>That the letter sent to her at 08:12 asked the wrong question. The link is her <strong>grandmother</strong>, not her grandfather, and the chain from the register to the Baldock censuses, laid out so she can check it.</td>
        <td>Anything the family holds about <strong>Cynthia&rsquo;s own people</strong> — and who she knew in Johannesburg before she got there, because a young woman does not sail from Hertfordshire alone and by chance.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p class="small">
    <strong>Neither was a new approach.</strong> Both went into threads opened in June 2025. The second
    exists because a wrong question had been put in writing that morning, and leaving it standing would
    have been worse than sending twice in one day.
  </p>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>And a sentence in both of them is already overstated <span class="tag disp">Noted, not resent</span></h3>
    <p>
      Both messages say Derrick&rsquo;s father was <em>most likely a son</em> of George and Caroline.
      An hour later that was <a href={base + '/dna/#rung'}>walked back</a> — the generation is not
      settled. <strong>No third message has been sent.</strong> The question actually asked of them
      &mdash; what became of the Castle sons, and did any go to South Africa &mdash; is the right
      question under <em>either</em> reading, because under the further one Derrick&rsquo;s father is a
      son of one of those sons. Only the confidence of one sentence was wrong, and three messages to the
      same two people in one morning would cost more than it corrected. It will be put right in the
      reply, if a reply comes.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Replies sent into the June 2025 threads</h2>
  <div class="scroll"><table>
    <thead><tr><th>To</th><th>Shared with Derrick</th><th>The one thing asked</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>The closest match</strong></td><td>297.5 cM</td><td><strong>Who were Mervin Halsey&rsquo;s parents, and where was he born?</strong> Either Mervin was a brother of Derrick&rsquo;s biological father, or Mervin&rsquo;s parent was</td></tr>
